QALL.push({"id":"r1","d":"R","t":9,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"Your latest status review shows the project is consuming budget faster than it is completing work, and the trend has held for three reporting periods.","q":"What should the project manager do first?","o":["Request additional funding to cover the projected shortfall","Analyze the variance to determine its cause before proposing a response","Reduce scope to bring the remaining work within the current budget","Report the trend to the sponsor and ask how they would like to proceed"],"c":1,"e":["Asking for money before you know why it's being consumed means you may fund a problem instead of fixing it.","Correct. Measure, analyze, then act. A three-period trend is a signal to diagnose — the response depends entirely on the cause.","Cutting scope is one possible response, chosen after analysis, not instead of it.","Reporting is necessary, but arriving with a variance and no analysis or recommendation is the escalation trap."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r2","d":"R","t":8,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"A critical path activity finishes eight days late. The project completion date is contractually fixed.","q":"What should the project manager do first?","o":["Fast track the remaining work by overlapping the next two activities","Crash the schedule by adding resources to downstream critical path activities","Analyze the schedule variance and evaluate the available compression options","Notify the customer that the completion date is at risk"],"c":2,"e":["Fast tracking adds rework risk and may not even be feasible on these particular activities. It's a candidate option, not a first move.","Crashing adds cost and only works where added resources actually compress duration. You'd choose it after comparing it against alternatives.","Correct. Analyze the variation, then evaluate compression options against cost, risk, and feasibility. The technique is selected, not reached for.","Premature. You do not yet know whether the date is genuinely at risk, and you'd be reporting a problem with no analysis or recommendation."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r3","d":"R","t":2,"m":"pred","r":"change","s":"A team member tells you they added a small usability improvement while working on an approved feature. It took two hours and the customer will like it.","q":"What should the project manager do?","o":["Thank them and note the improvement in the status report","Address the unapproved change and route it through the change control process","Ask the customer whether they want to keep the improvement","Remove the change immediately and restore the approved design"],"c":1,"e":["Praising unapproved work, however small and however well intended, tells the team that scope is theirs to add. This is textbook gold plating.","Correct. Size and goodwill are irrelevant. Unapproved work gets identified, assessed for impact, and routed through change control like any other change.","Asking the customer to bless work that was never approved puts them in an awkward position and still bypasses the process.","Ripping it out unilaterally is as much an uncontrolled change as adding it was. The process decides, not you."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r4","d":"R","t":3,"m":"agile","r":"value","s":"Midway through delivery, market conditions change and a large backlog item your team has been preparing for will no longer produce the benefit stated in the business case.","q":"What should the project manager do?","o":["Complete the item since it is already in the backlog and partially prepared","Work with the product owner to reprioritize the backlog against current value","Remove the item from the backlog to prevent wasted effort","Escalate to the sponsor for a decision on whether to continue"],"c":1,"e":["Sunk preparation is not a reason to deliver something that no longer produces value. The backlog exists precisely so it can change.","Correct. Value is examined throughout the project, and prioritization by value with the product owner is the adaptive mechanism for acting on it.","The direction is right but the actor is wrong — the product owner owns backlog content, not the project manager.","This is a normal reprioritization within the team's authority, not a decision that needs the sponsor."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r5","d":"R","t":7,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"Quality inspections have found the same defect type recurring across three consecutive deliverables, each caught and fixed before release.","q":"What should the project manager do?","o":["Increase inspection frequency to catch the defects earlier","Conduct a root cause analysis and implement a preventive improvement","Add the rework hours to the cost of quality and continue","Assign a dedicated quality reviewer to the affected work stream"],"c":1,"e":["More inspection finds the same defects more often. It's detection, not prevention, and the cost of quality keeps climbing.","Correct. A recurring defect is a process problem. Root cause analysis and continuous improvement address the source rather than the symptom.","Accounting for the waste accurately is not the same as reducing it. This accepts a known, repeating, preventable cost.","Adding a reviewer is more appraisal cost layered onto a process that keeps producing the same failure."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r6","d":"R","t":5,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"A key vendor has missed two consecutive delivery milestones. Their contract includes performance clauses you have not yet invoked.","q":"What should the project manager do first?","o":["Invoke the penalty clause to establish that the terms will be enforced","Begin sourcing a replacement vendor in parallel","Evaluate the vendor's performance against the agreement and meet to understand the cause","Escalate to procurement to manage the contractual relationship"],"c":2,"e":["Penalties are a lever, not a diagnosis. Invoking them before you understand the cause can damage a relationship you still depend on.","Dual-sourcing is expensive and adds risk. It's a response to consider once you know whether this is recoverable.","Correct. Evaluate vendor performance, then manage the supplier relationship. Understand the cause before you select a contractual response.","Procurement supports you, but managing supplier performance on your project is your task."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r7","d":"R","t":1,"m":"hybrid","r":"mode","s":"You are starting a project where the business outcome is well understood but the technical solution is uncertain and expected to evolve as the team learns.","q":"Which development approach should the project manager recommend?","o":["Predictive, because the business outcome is clearly defined","An adaptive or hybrid approach that allows the solution to emerge iteratively","Predictive with a change control board to handle solution changes","Defer the decision until the technical uncertainty is resolved"],"c":1,"e":["A clear outcome does not mean a clear solution. Baselining an uncertain solution guarantees a stream of change requests.","Correct. High solution uncertainty with a stable outcome is the standard case for iterative delivery. Assess needs and complexity, then recommend the approach.","Using change control as a substitute for an approach suited to uncertainty means managing churn rather than reducing it.","Deferring the approach decision leaves the team without a way of working. The uncertainty is exactly what the approach must accommodate."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r8","d":"R","t":6,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"Your project's contingency reserve is 60% consumed at the 30% completion point. No new risks have been identified.","q":"What should the project manager do?","o":["Request additional contingency reserve from the sponsor","Analyze what is consuming the reserve and reassess the risk provision","Transfer funds from the management reserve to contingency","Reduce the remaining scope to protect the budget"],"c":1,"e":["A reserve request with no analysis behind it is asking the sponsor to fund a problem neither of you understands.","Correct. Monitor financial variations and manage reserves — which begins with understanding why the provision is being drawn down faster than the work is progressing.","Management reserve covers unknown-unknowns and is not yours to move. It also treats a symptom.","Cutting scope before diagnosing the cost driver may remove work that isn't the problem."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r9","d":"R","t":10,"m":"pred","r":"assess","s":"All deliverables are complete and accepted, but the operations team says they are not ready to take over support.","q":"What should the project manager do?","o":["Close the project since all deliverables have been formally accepted","Validate readiness for transition and complete the activities required to close the phase","Extend the project schedule until the operations team is ready","Escalate to the sponsor that operations is blocking closure"],"c":1,"e":["Acceptance of deliverables is not the same as readiness for transition. Closing here strands the product and the support team.","Correct. Validating readiness for transition to operations is an explicit part of managing closure. Acceptance and handover are separate criteria.","Extending the schedule without knowing what readiness actually requires is an open-ended commitment.","Operations raising a legitimate readiness gap is not obstruction, and escalating it treats a closure task as someone else's failure."],"k":"mcq"},{"id":"r10","d":"R","t":4,"m":"hybrid","r":"assess","s":"Two of your six team members will be pulled to a higher-priority initiative for the next four weeks. You learn this from a colleague, not formally.","q":"What should the project manager do first?","o":["Confirm the resource change and assess its impact on the schedule and deliverables","Immediately escalate to the sponsor to retain the resources","Rebaseline the schedule to reflect the reduced capacity","Ask the remaining team members to absorb the additional work"],"c":0,"e":["Correct. Verify the information, then assess impact. Acting on secondhand news before confirming it risks responding to something that isn't happening.","Escalating on a rumor, with no impact assessment, gives the sponsor nothing to act on and costs you credibility.","Rebaselining is a change control action taken after impact analysis and approval, not a reflex.","Silently loading the remaining team is how burnout and quality failures start, and it hides a real resource issue from the people who need to see it."],"k":"mcq"});
